{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small mobile phone repair shop in Nairobi with 3 employees. We fix phones, replace screens, and sell used phone parts. What regulatory obligations do I need to be aware of?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "I'm unable to provide a complete answer to your question because the source material provided to me is corrupted and unreadable. To give you accurate guidance on your specific shop's regulatory obligations—whether relating to business registration, tax compliance, employee matters, consumer protection, or product liability for parts you sell—I would need access to clear, legible regulatory documentation. I recommend contacting the Kenya Revenue Authority (KRA) for tax obligations, the National Treasury for licensing requirements, and the County Commissioner's office for local business registration requirements specific to your area."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "kenya_single_business_permit_guide.txt", "source_text": "�q���\u0014\u0001�m�n��F۪Y-֖d�7�\u0018�@ʫ���\u001b~�.��;�0�FKئ:s�4�F�e�zc�1V- D&3��Yb ns]m-\u0016\u0003�����YD���y��+�6�k \u0005��\u0000��'ֺ��>��ʓ�`f�iok�ꚉ�\u0019o�@ L\u0014ظ���M)���j�pRKE���0��?�i��9�\u000eZ��j\u0016M{���>�F����Æ9��$��hӼ'yf��\\k2M��%X�#F���#k��(' WSҖ�����\u0005�i�26�\bͫ[�%��F�O�uf��-��.-����\u0016��\u0017\u0016�\u0005k\u001b1 6��!��>ٮ�Ҋ,\u0017g!m��.�P����{�-��mD\u0011�񜫄���SN��}������UI�+Ԏ/��}�\u001a#\u0006�\u0017=�]h\u0014z�`3�]C�v�G��<��(�I����1ֱ�|1� KQ��_� \u001a���m��������1]M\u0003�`r7�\u0006F\u0016�L���[�����U�2�a�a�O>��K��^H� F�c{D�c=���h�P�\u0004\u0007�\u0005vTf���| o\u0013Q\u0012�Ǻ�;Q\u001b�9��\u0011��'��zzSm�!:Y�s_�g7��;Cj\"@��\u0000\u0002��\u00003]X�Fz� �;"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm a sole trader running a small retail shop in Nairobi. Last year I registered my business name with the county, but I've just realized the name is very similar to another established shop down the street. I'm worried the registration might not have been proper. What should I do about this?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "If your business name registration shouldn't have been approved in the first place—whether because it's too similar to an existing business or for other reasons—you need to take action now. The law recognizes that registrations can happen by mistake, and it covers situations exactly like yours where a name got registered when it shouldn't have been. The key is to notify the registration authority of the problem and request a change to your business name. Don't wait on this—the sooner you correct the registration, the better. Get in touch with your county business registration office and explain the issue so they can guide you through updating your registered business name to something properly distinct."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "business_registration_act_cap499.pdf", "source_text": "ation of change to that business name. (4) If, after the commencement of this Act— (a) any firm, individual or corporation is, through inadvertence or otherwise, registered under a business name under which registration under this Act ought to have been refused; or (b) any change of ownership of a business occurs as a result of which a firm, individual or corporation is carrying on business under a business name which, on an application for registration under this Act, ought to have been refused"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm running a small retail shop in Nairobi with 3 employees and annual turnover around 2 million shillings. Do I need to work with a CPA or accountant, and what should I know about professional accounting standards in Kenya?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "For a retail business your size, you don't automatically need a CPA on staff, but you should understand that Kenya has a regulated accountancy profession overseen by ICPAK—the Institute of Certified Public Accountants of Kenya. ICPAK sets standards and promotes best practices for accounting in the country. If you decide to hire an accountant or get accounting services, make sure they're working to standards that uphold public interest and professional conduct. You can reach ICPAK at +254 719 074 000 or memberservices@icpak.com if you need to verify someone's credentials or get guidance on what good accounting practice looks like for your business size."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "icpak_about_2026.txt", "source_text": "Menu To develop, regulate and promote an internationally recognized accountancy profession that upholds public interest through capacity building, advocacy, innovation and sustainable practices. Contact Information P.O BOX 59963-00200 CPA Center, Ruaraka, Thika road. Nairobi, Kenya. Telephone Line: +254 719 074 000 Email: memberservices@icpak.com Facebook Linkedin Youtube Whatsapp Member Of Crafted with Brait Consulting Limited Quick Links Lodge a Complaint Fire Awards FAQs Portal Login Find A C"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the main regulatory requirements that mortgage finance companies need to follow in Kenya?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Mortgage finance companies in Kenya must comply with prudential requirements and market conduct rules. These requirements exist to protect the stability and soundness of the overall financial system. The regulatory framework ensures that these institutions maintain adequate financial positions and operate fairly with customers. This oversight applies alongside the framework that governs commercial banks, creating a coordinated system for managing lending institutions in the country."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "d mortgage finance companies are subject to regulatory requirements governing their prudential position and market conduct in order to safeguard the overall soundness and stability of the financial system. Commercial Banks and Mortgage Finance Companies https://www.centralbank.go.ke/contact-us/ The Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008 set out the legal, regulatory and supervisory framework for the microfinance industry in Kenya. The Microfinan"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small savings group in Nairobi with about 15 members who pool money monthly and give loans to each other. We're not registered with the Central Bank yet. Do the microfinance regulations apply to us, and what should we know?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Your savings group operates in a space where microfinance regulations become relevant once you start taking deposits or offering formal lending services. The Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008 set the legal and regulatory framework for microfinance operations in Kenya, including supervision requirements. If your group is collecting deposits from members and providing loans, you may fall under these regulations depending on how formally you are structured and whether you are taking deposits from non-members. The key point is that once a microfinance institution takes deposits, it must comply with prudential requirements—rules designed to keep the institution financially sound—and market conduct standards to protect members and maintain stability. You should register with the Central Bank and understand which specific regulatory requirements apply to your group's structure before expanding operations."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "d mortgage finance companies are subject to regulatory requirements governing their prudential position and market conduct in order to safeguard the overall soundness and stability of the financial system. Commercial Banks and Mortgage Finance Companies https://www.centralbank.go.ke/contact-us/ The Microfinance Act 2006 and the Microfinance (Deposit Taking Institutions) Regulations 2008 set out the legal, regulatory and supervisory framework for the microfinance industry in Kenya. The Microfinan"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm starting a small wholesale business with two other people. We each want to put in 50,000 shillings to get going, but we're worried about losing more than that if the business runs into trouble. What type of company structure should we consider, and how does it protect us?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You should register as a private company limited by shares. Here's why: each of you will become a shareholder, and your personal liability is limited strictly to the amount you've invested — in your case, 50,000 shillings each. If the business accumulates debts or faces a lawsuit, your personal assets beyond that 50,000 shillings cannot be touched. You won't be personally responsible for the full debt. This is the standard setup for small businesses in Kenya where multiple owners want to share risk while protecting their personal wealth. Make sure you register with the Registrar of Companies to formalize this protection."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "o limit on the liability of its members Public limited company A public company has share capital and limits the liability of each member to the amount unpaid on their shares. It may offer its shares for sale to the general public and may be listed at the securities Company limited by guarantee This type of company does not have share capital and its members are guarantors rather than shareholders. The liability of members is limited to the amount they agree to contribute to the company’s assets"}}
