{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I have a small retail shop that I've been running as a sole proprietor for two years under a business name. Last month, my cousin bought a 40% stake in the business as a silent partner, so technically the ownership has changed. Do I need to update my business name registration with the authorities?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, you need to update your business name registration. When there's a change of ownership in your business—even if it's a partial stake being sold to a new owner—you're required to inform the registration authority. The registration rules are clear that whenever ownership changes hands, the business name registration status has to be reviewed and updated accordingly. This is important because your business name registration is tied to who actually owns and controls the business at any given time. Contact the business registration office right away to notify them of your cousin's stake in the shop and get the records updated."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "brs_registration_business_names_act.pdf", "source_text": "after the commencement of this Act— 12 [Rev. 2019] CAP. 499 Registration of Business Names (a) any firm, individual or corporation is, through inadvertence or otherwise, registered under a business name under which registration under this Act ought to have been refused; or (b) any change of ownership of a business occurs as a result of which a firm, individual or corporation is carrying on business under a business name which, on an application for registration under this Act, ought to have bee"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm starting a retail shop as a sole proprietor in Kenya. Walk me through the steps I need to follow to register my business name properly and avoid any issues with the registration authorities."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's what you need to do to register your business name correctly:\n\n1. **Choose your business name carefully** — Make sure the name you pick is one that would be approved if you applied for registration under the Business Names Act. Avoid names that are too similar to existing registered businesses or that might be refused for other regulatory reasons.\n\n2. **Check before you register** — Confirm that no other business is already using the name you want. This prevents you from being registered under a name that should have been refused in the first place.\n\n3. **Complete your registration application** — Submit your business name registration through the proper channels with accurate information about yourself as the sole proprietor.\n\n4. **Keep your registration current** — If your business changes ownership or structure later, you must update your registration. If you sell the business or bring in a partner, the new owner or partnership structure needs to be registered under the correct business name.\n\n5. **Avoid registration under an improper name** — Don't attempt to operate under a business name that wouldn't qualify for registration. The authorities can challenge this, and you could face complications or be required to re-register under a compliant name.\n\nThe key is getting it right from the start — choose a registrable name, apply correctly, and update immediately if ownership changes. You can confirm the specific current registration requirements with the Business Registration office to ensure you meet all current standards."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "brs_registration_business_names_act.pdf", "source_text": "after the commencement of this Act— 12 [Rev. 2019] CAP. 499 Registration of Business Names (a) any firm, individual or corporation is, through inadvertence or otherwise, registered under a business name under which registration under this Act ought to have been refused; or (b) any change of ownership of a business occurs as a result of which a firm, individual or corporation is carrying on business under a business name which, on an application for registration under this Act, ought to have bee"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small handicraft business in Nairobi making leather goods. I want to start exporting my products to Uganda next month. Do I need a Certificate of Origin, and who do I get it from?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, you need a Certificate of Origin for your leather goods exports. The Kenya Chamber of Commerce and Industry (KNCCI) is now the authority issuing these certificates for local products like yours. You'll apply through them rather than going to Kenya Bureau of Standards. Since you're just starting exports, KEPROBA (Kenya Export Promotion and Branding Agency) can also walk you through the basic export requirements and help you understand what documents you'll need to prepare alongside the COO. Get in touch with the Chamber directly through their website to start the application process."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "mber issues Certificates of Origin to the local products. The Certificate of Origin (COO) in Kenya has been issued by Kenya Bureau of Standards (KEBS) until recently when it was reverted back to the Chamber in the government effort to revive it. General Export Certificate of origin https://www.kenyachamber.or.ke/ kncci-services/ Kenya Export promotion and Bran ding Agency KEPROBA provides basic information on export requirements General Export Preparing and processing export documents -Basic exp"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What do I need to know about getting a Certificate of Origin for my products if I want to export them from Kenya?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "A Certificate of Origin (COO) is your official document proving your products are made in Kenya. The Kenya National Chamber of Commerce and Industry (KNCCI) now issues these certificates — they recently took over responsibility from the Kenya Bureau of Standards (KEBS). When you're ready to export, KNCCI is where you'll apply. You can also get basic guidance on export requirements and help preparing your export documents through the Kenya Export Promotion and Branding Agency (KEPROBA). Start by contacting KNCCI directly through their services section to understand the specific process for your product type."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "mber issues Certificates of Origin to the local products. The Certificate of Origin (COO) in Kenya has been issued by Kenya Bureau of Standards (KEBS) until recently when it was reverted back to the Chamber in the government effort to revive it. General Export Certificate of origin https://www.kenyachamber.or.ke/ kncci-services/ Kenya Export promotion and Bran ding Agency KEPROBA provides basic information on export requirements General Export Preparing and processing export documents -Basic exp"}}
