{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What's the main purpose of the Microfinance Act 2006 in Kenya, and when did it start applying?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "The Microfinance Act 2006 came into effect on May 2, 2008, and its core job is to regulate how deposit-taking microfinance institutions are set up, run, and operated in Kenya. The Act does this through a licensing and supervision system managed by the Central Bank of Kenya. One of the key things it allows is for licensed microfinance banks to take deposits from the public, which helps promote savings in the economy. In short: it's the framework that ensures microfinance banks operate safely and fairly."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb1_legal_regulatory", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "doing_business_kenya_launch.txt", "source_text": "e legal, regulatory and supervisory framework for the microfinance industry in Kenya. The Microfinance Act 2006 became operational with effect from May 2, 2008. The principal object of the Microfinance Act 2006 is to regulate the establishment, business and operations of deposit taking microfinance institutions in Kenya through licensing and supervision. The Act enables microfinance banks (MFBs) licensed by the Central Bank of Kenya to mobilise deposits from the general public, thus promoting sa"}}
