{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I need a checklist of the steps I should follow if I want to request more time to pay my taxes to KRA. What's the process?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's what you need to know about requesting extra time to pay your taxes:\n\n1. **Understand your option** — KRA has a formal process for extending your tax payment deadline. This isn't automatic, so you need to request it.\n\n2. **Submit your request early** — Don't wait until your payment is due. Contact KRA in advance with your situation and ask for an extension of time to pay.\n\n3. **Prepare your case** — Be ready to explain why you need more time (cash flow challenges, seasonal business, unexpected expense, etc.).\n\n4. **Follow KRA's submission process** — You can apply through KRA's online portal, by email to your KRA office, or by visiting in person. Ask your KRA compliance officer which method they prefer.\n\n5. **Keep records** — Once approved, keep the extension letter or confirmation number. This protects you from penalties if you pay after the original deadline but within your new one.\n\n6. **Meet the new deadline** — The extended deadline is binding. Missing it still attracts penalties, so make sure you can meet it before requesting.\n\nTax payment extensions are meant for genuine hardship, not routine delays. The sooner you request, the better your chances of approval. Get the current contact details and application form from your local KRA office to start."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "tax_procedures_act_cap469b.pdf", "source_text": ".......................................... 22 32A. Mutual administrative assistance in the recovery or collection tax claims ................................................................ 22 33. Extension of time to pay tax ......................................................................................................................................................... 23 34. Priority of tax ................................................................................................."}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Create a step-by-step checklist for how my small retail business should handle foreign exchange gains or losses when I buy stock from international suppliers. I need to know what I'm supposed to track and report to KRA."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's what you need to do when your business realises foreign exchange gains or losses:\n\n1. **Track all foreign currency transactions** — Keep records of every purchase or sale you make in foreign currency, including the exchange rate on the transaction date.\n\n2. **Calculate your gain or loss** — When you settle the transaction (pay the supplier or receive payment), compare the exchange rate you used when you first recorded it to the actual rate when you paid. The difference is your foreign exchange gain or loss.\n\n3. **Separate this from your normal business income** — Foreign exchange gains and losses are treated as a distinct category of business income under Kenyan tax law, not just lumped into your general profit figure.\n\n4. **Report it correctly on your tax return** — When you file your income tax return with KRA, you'll declare this foreign exchange gain or loss in the appropriate section for business income from foreign exchange realisation.\n\n5. **Keep supporting documents** — Hold onto invoices, payment receipts, bank statements, and exchange rate records so you can justify the amounts to KRA if needed.\n\nThis matters because KRA treats foreign exchange separately from your core business profit, so you need to show them the calculation clearly. Keep your records organised by transaction so you can report accurately each year."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "income_tax_act_cap470.pdf", "source_text": ".......................... 16 4A. Income from businesses where foreign exchange loss or gain is realized ................................................................... 17 4B. Export processing zone enterprise ............................................................................................................................................... 18 5. Income from employment, etc. ..........................................................................................................."}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the main things I need to do as a business owner to stay compliant with VAT payments to KRA?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "You have three core responsibilities: file your tax returns on time, pay any VAT due by the deadline, and pay any penalties or interest if they apply to your account. When you're ready to pay, use the iTax portal at https://itax.kra.go.ke with your PIN and password. Once logged in, go to the Payments tab and select Payment Registration. Then work through these selections: Tax head as VAT, Tax Subhead as VAT, Payment Type as Self-Assessment, then pick your tax period and add the liability. For payment method, you can use either Other Payment Mode or RGTS. Submit and you're done. The key is doing this on schedule—don't wait until the last minute, as late payments trigger penalties and interest charges that add to what you already owe."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "kra_vat.txt", "source_text": "e. File tax returns on time Pay tax due on time. Pay penalties and interest (where applicable). Step by step procedure for VAT Payment Login to iTax using your PIN and password via https://itax.kra.go.ke . Click on Payments tab, select Payment Registration. Select Tax head as VAT Select Tax Subhead as VAT Select Payment Type as Self-Assessment. Select Tax Period Select the liability and click on add. Select Mode of Payment as Other Payment Mode or RGTS. Click on the submit button. The system wil"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small retail shop in Nairobi selling clothes and shoes. I'm registered for VAT. My business made sales of about 500,000 shillings last month. Do I need to file a VAT return and pay VAT, and if so, how do I do it?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, you must file a VAT return and pay any VAT you owe on time. Here's how to pay your VAT through iTax: Log in to iTax at https://itax.kra.go.ke using your PIN and password. Go to the Payments tab and select Payment Registration. Then choose VAT as your tax head, VAT as your tax subhead, and Self-Assessment as your payment type. Select the tax period that applies to your sales, add the VAT liability amount, and choose your payment mode—either Other Payment Mode or RGTS. Submit and complete the payment. Make sure you pay by the deadline to avoid penalties and interest charges. Confirm your VAT filing deadlines and payment dates with KRA to stay on track."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "kra_vat.txt", "source_text": "e. File tax returns on time Pay tax due on time. Pay penalties and interest (where applicable). Step by step procedure for VAT Payment Login to iTax using your PIN and password via https://itax.kra.go.ke . Click on Payments tab, select Payment Registration. Select Tax head as VAT Select Tax Subhead as VAT Select Payment Type as Self-Assessment. Select Tax Period Select the liability and click on add. Select Mode of Payment as Other Payment Mode or RGTS. Click on the submit button. The system wil"}}
