{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm importing agricultural seeds into Kenya—things like sunflower seeds, cotton seeds, and sesame seeds. Can you give me a checklist of the tariff codes I need to use when declaring these imports to KRA?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's your tariff code checklist for seed imports:\n\n**Oilseeds & Legume Seeds:**\n- Soybeans (not broken): 1201.90.00\n- Groundnuts (shelled or not): 1202.42.00\n- Copra: 1203.00.00\n- Linseed (broken or whole): 1204.00.00\n- Low erucic acid rape/colza seeds: 1205.10.00\n- Other rape/colza seeds: 1205.90.00\n\n**Oil-bearing Seeds:**\n- Sunflower seeds (broken or whole): 1206.00.00\n- Cotton seeds (broken or whole): 1207.21.00 or 1207.29.00\n- Sesamum/sesame seeds (broken or whole): 1207.40.00\n\nUse the exact tariff number that matches your import on your KRA declaration form. If your seeds are broken or processed differently, check whether the \"broken\" or \"whole\" code applies. Confirm the precise tariff classification for your specific shipment with KRA before submitting your import declaration."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "vat_act_cap476.pdf", "source_text": "ot broken of tariff No. 1202.42.00. 7. Copra of tariff No. 1203.00.00. 8. Linseed, whether or not broken of tariff No. 1204.00.00 9. Low erucic acid rape or colza seeds of tariff No. 1205.10.00. 10. Other rape or colza seeds of tariff No. 1205.90.00. 11. Sunflower seeds, whether or not broken of tariff No. 1206.00.00. 12. Cotton seeds, whether or not broken of tariff Nos. 1207.21.00 and 1207.29.00. 13. Sesamum seeds, whether or not broken of tariff No. 1207.40.00. 23 Value Added Tax Act (Cap. 47"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm a small business owner and I've set up a trust structure for my business. What's the key rule I need to know about how trustees can designate dividends and interest as 'qualifying'?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's what matters: A trustee can designate amounts received as dividends or interest as 'qualifying' — and once designated that way, they're treated as already tax-paid. But there's a critical limit: the total amount a trustee designates as qualifying dividends cannot exceed the actual qualifying dividends the trust has received, and the same applies separately for qualifying interest. In plain terms, you can't designate more than what actually came in. If your trust received 100,000 in qualifying dividends, you can designate up to 100,000 as qualifying — no more. This keeps the trust honest with the tax authorities. Confirm the current qualifying dividend and interest thresholds with KRA to make sure your designations stay compliant."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "income_tax_act_cap470.pdf", "source_text": "to tax under subsection (2) to be qualifying dividends or qualifying interest and, in that case, such designated amount shall be deemed to have been already tax paid. 29 Income Tax Act (Cap. 470) Kenya (5) The cumulative totals, at any time, of the amounts designated up to that time by a trustee under subsection (4) as qualifying dividends or qualifying interest shall not exceed the cumulative totals of qualifying dividends or qualifying interest respectively, received by the trustee, in his cap"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small retail shop as a sole proprietor. I've just inherited some money and my uncle says I should put it in a trust to avoid taxes on the dividends and interest I'll earn. Will that work under Kenyan tax law?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "No, that won't work the way your uncle is suggesting. Under Kenyan tax law, a trustee can only designate amounts as qualifying dividends or qualifying interest up to the actual amount of qualifying dividends and interest the trust has actually received. You can't designate more than what came in. And here's the key point: even when a trustee does designate amounts as qualifying dividends or qualifying interest, those amounts are treated as already tax-paid, meaning you'll still owe tax on them—you just get credit for the tax already paid. So putting inherited money into a trust doesn't shield you from tax on the investment income it generates. You'll pay tax on the dividends and interest either way. Speak with a tax advisor or the KRA to understand the specific tax treatment that applies to your inheritance and investment plans."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "income_tax_act_cap470.pdf", "source_text": "to tax under subsection (2) to be qualifying dividends or qualifying interest and, in that case, such designated amount shall be deemed to have been already tax paid. 29 Income Tax Act (Cap. 470) Kenya (5) The cumulative totals, at any time, of the amounts designated up to that time by a trustee under subsection (4) as qualifying dividends or qualifying interest shall not exceed the cumulative totals of qualifying dividends or qualifying interest respectively, received by the trustee, in his cap"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What's the basic rule about input tax deduction for my registered business?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "As a registered person, you can deduct the input tax you've paid on business supplies and imports from the output tax you owe. This happens at the end of each tax period when you file your return. The key is that you must be registered and the supplies must be for your taxable business activities. Make sure you keep proper documentation of all input tax paid, as you'll need to report this correctly in your tax returns to claim the deduction."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "vat_act_cap476.pdf", "source_text": "t note issued under this section shall be in the prescribed form. [Act No. 10 of 2018, s. 15, Act No. 2 of 2020, Sch.] Part VI – DEDUCTION OF INPUT TAX 17. Credit for input tax against output tax (1) Subject to the provisions of this Act and the regulations, input tax on a taxable supply to, or importation made by, a registered person may, at the end of the tax period in which the supply or importation occurred, be deducted by the registered person in a return for the period, subject to the exce"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small retail shop and I'm registered for VAT. Can you give me a step-by-step guide on how to claim back the input tax I've paid on my business purchases each month?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's how to claim your input tax credits as a registered VAT business:\n\n1. **Keep all your invoices and receipts** – Collect and store every receipt from suppliers showing the VAT you paid on business purchases (stock, equipment, supplies).\n\n2. **Identify your input tax amount** – Look at each invoice for the VAT charged. This is the input tax you're entitled to claim back.\n\n3. **Fill out your VAT return** – At the end of each tax period (usually monthly), complete your KRA VAT return form and list all your input tax claims.\n\n4. **Deduct input tax from output tax** – On your return, subtract the total input tax you paid from the total output tax (VAT) you collected from customers. This gives you your net VAT liability or refund.\n\n5. **Submit to KRA** – File your completed return with the deduction claimed.\n\nThe key rule: you can only claim input tax on supplies and imports related to your taxable business activity. Keep your documentation organized by tax period so KRA can verify your claims if needed. Check with KRA on the specific return deadlines that apply to your registration."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "vat_act_cap476.pdf", "source_text": "t note issued under this section shall be in the prescribed form. [Act No. 10 of 2018, s. 15, Act No. 2 of 2020, Sch.] Part VI – DEDUCTION OF INPUT TAX 17. Credit for input tax against output tax (1) Subject to the provisions of this Act and the regulations, input tax on a taxable supply to, or importation made by, a registered person may, at the end of the tax period in which the supply or importation occurred, be deducted by the registered person in a return for the period, subject to the exce"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small oil marketing business in Nairobi and I'm registered for VAT. I need a checklist of which business expenses I can and cannot claim input tax on. Can you create a simple guide?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here's what you need to know about claiming input tax on your expenses:\n\n**Expenses WHERE YOU CANNOT claim input tax:**\n- Acquisition of vehicles or equipment\n- Leasing or hiring of vehicles or equipment\n\n**Important timing note for your business:**\nIf you were operating before new VAT rules came into effect, you have 12 months from that date to claim any input tax you paid on supplies before those rules kicked in. Don't miss this window.\n\n**What this means practically:**\nWhen you buy fuel, spare parts, or services for your marketing operations, check whether the expense falls into the restricted categories above. If it does—for example, if you're buying a delivery vehicle or leasing storage space—you cannot deduct that VAT. For other legitimate business purchases (office supplies, fuel for operations, maintenance services), you can claim the input tax as normal.\n\nKeep all your invoices organized by category so you know exactly which expenses qualify. Your accountant or the KRA can clarify any borderline cases for your specific transactions."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "vat_act_cap476.pdf", "source_text": "ing the name and PIN of the winner of the tender and the name of the other oil marketing company participating in the tender: Provided that the input tax that may have been incurred by an oil marketing company participating in the Open Tender System before the coming into force of this provision shall be claimed within twelve months after this provision comes into force. (4) A registered person shall not deduct input tax under this Act if the tax relates to the acquisition, leasing or hiring of—"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small fuel distribution business and I'm registered for VAT. Last year, before the new Open Tender System rules came into effect, I paid input tax on fuel leasing costs. Can I claim that input tax now?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Yes, you can claim that input tax, but you need to act quickly. You have twelve months from the date the Open Tender System provision came into force to submit your claim for any input tax you paid on leasing or hiring costs before that date. After the twelve-month window closes, you won't be able to recover that input tax. Get your invoices and records together and file your claim with KRA before the deadline expires."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "vat_act_cap476.pdf", "source_text": "ing the name and PIN of the winner of the tender and the name of the other oil marketing company participating in the tender: Provided that the input tax that may have been incurred by an oil marketing company participating in the Open Tender System before the coming into force of this provision shall be claimed within twelve months after this provision comes into force. (4) A registered person shall not deduct input tax under this Act if the tax relates to the acquisition, leasing or hiring of—"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What should I know about how the KRA handles information when they examine my business records?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "When KRA examiners come to review your records, they can make copies of your documents for their examination reports. The key point for you: any information they collect during that examination is confidential and can only be used for tax purposes—nothing else. They can ask you to provide information on oath, either verbally or in writing, and you need to comply with that request. So don't worry that confidential business details will leak; the law binds them to keep it private and use it only for tax matters."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "tax_procedures_act_cap469b.pdf", "source_text": "n may make copies of such documents for the purposes of any report relating to the examination; and (c) the confidentiality of the information obtained in the course of the examination by the Commissioner or authorised officer shall be maintained and the information shall be used solely for the purposes of the tax laws. (3) The Commissioner or authorised officer may require that the information referred to in subsection (1) be— (a) given on oath, verbally or in writing, and, for that purpose, th"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I'm a small retail shop owner in Nairobi and I need to understand what tax obligations I have to the KRA. Can you give me a step-by-step checklist of the main taxes I should be aware of and filing requirements?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Here are the core tax obligations you need to track as a retail shop owner:\n\n1. **Get your KRA PIN** – This is your first step. Register as an individual with the KRA if you haven't already.\n\n2. **Pay As You Earn (PAYE)** – If you have employees, you must deduct PAYE from their salaries and remit it to KRA.\n\n3. **Value Added Tax (VAT)** – Depending on your turnover, you may need to register for VAT and file VAT returns. This applies to goods you sell.\n\n4. **Turnover Tax (TOT)** – If your annual turnover is below the VAT threshold, you may be eligible for TOT instead, which is simpler.\n\n5. **Withholding Tax** – If you receive payments from certain sources, tax may be withheld and you need to account for this.\n\n6. **Individual Income Tax** – File annual income tax returns based on your shop's net profit.\n\n7. **Tax Compliance Certificate (TCC)** – Once compliant, you can apply for a TCC, which is often required for business licenses and contracts.\n\n8. **File & Pay on Time** – Keep records of all transactions and meet KRA deadlines for filing and payment.\n\nStart by confirming your exact tax bracket and registration requirements with your nearest KRA office, as obligations depend on your annual turnover."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "drafting", "source_filename": "kra_etims_how_to_onboard.txt", "source_text": "er for a KRA PIN [Non-Individual ] How to Deregister your PIN as a Foreigner Requirements for PIN Deregistration of a Deceased Person Filing & Paying File & Pay --> Individual Income Tax Pay As You Earn (PAYE) Withholding Tax Tax Compliance Certificate (TCC) Installment Tax Advance Tax Rental Income Tax Capital Gains Tax Value Added Tax (VAT) Turnover Tax (TOT) Importing & Exporting Learn about Importation & Exportation --> Procedures for Motor Vehicle Other Goods How to Import Customs Bonds Spe"}}
{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "What are the main tax obligations and filing requirements I need to know about as a small business owner in Kenya?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "As a Kenyan business operator, you have several core tax responsibilities to manage. You need to register for a KRA PIN and maintain compliance through regular filing and payment of taxes. The key taxes you'll deal with include PAYE if you have employees, VAT on your sales, and potentially Turnover Tax depending on your business structure and income level. You may also have obligations around withholding tax if you make certain payments, rental income tax if you earn rental revenue, and capital gains tax on asset sales. If your business involves importing or exporting goods, you'll need to follow specific importation and exportation procedures including customs documentation. Beyond this, you'll need to maintain a Tax Compliance Certificate (TCC) to stay in good standing. You can file and pay your taxes through KRA's designated channels, and depending on your circumstances, you may be eligible for installment or advance tax arrangements. Make sure you verify your specific obligations with KRA based on your exact business type and income level."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb2_tax_kra", "task_type": "summarization", "source_filename": "kra_etims_how_to_onboard.txt", "source_text": "er for a KRA PIN [Non-Individual ] How to Deregister your PIN as a Foreigner Requirements for PIN Deregistration of a Deceased Person Filing & Paying File & Pay --> Individual Income Tax Pay As You Earn (PAYE) Withholding Tax Tax Compliance Certificate (TCC) Installment Tax Advance Tax Rental Income Tax Capital Gains Tax Value Added Tax (VAT) Turnover Tax (TOT) Importing & Exporting Learn about Importation & Exportation --> Procedures for Motor Vehicle Other Goods How to Import Customs Bonds Spe"}}
