{"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant advising Kenyan MSME operators on tax, registration, financing, and regulatory compliance."}, {"role": "user", "content": "I run a small craft workshop in Nairobi where I make and sell wooden furniture. I have 3 employees and we also do custom orders. What regulatory obligations should I be aware of regarding my business operations and cultural considerations in Kenya?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "Based on the regulatory framework for Kenyan MSMEs, your craft workshop is subject to several core obligations. First, you must register your business and obtain the necessary licenses from your county government—this is non-negotiable for a workshop with employees. Second, with 3 employees, you have statutory obligations under employment law: you must maintain written employment contracts, contribute to NSSF (National Social Security Fund), register with NHIF (National Hospital Insurance Fund), and comply with work safety standards in your workshop environment. Third, you must register for tax purposes with KRA and maintain proper business records for income tax, VAT if applicable, and any other relevant taxes. For a furniture business, ensure compliance with environmental regulations regarding waste disposal from your workshop. Since you do custom orders, maintain clear agreements with customers about delivery timelines and quality standards. Finally, respect intellectual property if you're adapting traditional designs—ensure you're not infringing on others' registered designs or trademarks. For your specific county and workshop type, confirm the exact licensing requirements with your county business office."}], "_meta": {"kb_id": "kb7_culture_context", "task_type": "analysis", "source_filename": "kenya_sme_business_plan_guide_2025.txt", "source_text": "; } content_single_page h1 a:hover{color: var(--jl-single-h1-color);} content_single_page h2 a:hover{color: var(--jl-single-h2-color);} cont